"[2602.02472] SPARKLING: Balancing Signal Preservation and Symmetry Breaking for Width-Progressive Learning (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02472)\nciteturn25771view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02472\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 2 Feb 2026 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 29 Jun 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:SPARKLING: Balancing Signal Preservation and Symmetry Breaking for Width-Progressive Learning\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Qifan Yu , cite7†Xinyu Ma , cite8†Zhijian Zhuo , cite9†Minrui Wang , cite10†Deyi Liu , cite11†Shiyi Zhan , cite12†Yiyuan Ma , cite13†Liang Xiang , cite14†Xingyan Bin , cite15†Di He L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled SPARKLING: Balancing Signal Preservation and Symmetry Breaking for Width-Progressive Learning, by Qifan Yu and 9 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite16†View PDF cite17†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Progressive Learning (PL) reduces pre-training computational overhead by gradually increasing model scale. While prior work has extensively explored depth expansion, width expansion remains significantly understudied, with the few existing methods limited to the early stages of training. However, expanding width during the mid-stage is essential for maximizing computational savings, yet it remains a formidable challenge due to severe training instabilities.\nL17: Empirically, we show that naive initialization at this stage disrupts activation statistics, triggering loss spikes, while copy-based initialization introduces gradient symmetry that hinders feature diversity. To address these issues, we propose SPARKLING (balancing {S}ignal {P}reservation {A}nd symmet{R}y brea{K}ing for width-progressive {L}earn{ING}), a novel framework for mid-stage width expansion.\nL18: Our method achieves signal preservation via RMS-scale consistency, stabilizing activation statistics during expansion. Symmetry breaking is ensured through asymmetric optimizer state reset and asymmetric learning rate re-warmup. Extensive experiments on dense and Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models demonstrate that, across multiple width axes and optimizer families, SPARKLING consistently outperforms training from scratch and reduces training cost by up to 35% under $2\\times$ width expansion.\nL19: Comments:  | ICML 2026 camera-ready version\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite18†arXiv:2602.02472 [cs.LG]\nL22:    | (or cite19†arXiv:2602.02472v2 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL23:    | cite20†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.02472†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Qifan Yu [cite21†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Mon, 2 Feb 2026 18:52:52 UTC (7,511 KB)\nL28: [v2] Mon, 29 Jun 2026 15:41:30 UTC (10,056 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled SPARKLING: Balancing Signal Preservation and Symmetry Breaking for Width-Progressive Learning, by Qifan Yu and 9 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite16†View PDF L37:   * cite17†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite22†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite23†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.LG\nL44: \nL45: cite24†< prev |   cite25†next > L46: \nL47: cite26†new | cite27†recent | cite28†2026-02 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite29†cs L52: cite30†cs.CL L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite31†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite32†Google Scholar†scholar.goo